{"changes":["eka-mcp v1.16.0 (internal/mcp): six advertised group handles — knowledge_read, knowledge_write, code, workspace, ops, feedback — each taking {\"op\":\"\u003cmember\u003e\",\"args\":{...}} and forwarding to the IDENTICAL flat handler (one code path; a group call and its flat alias return byte-identical content). Each group description embeds its members' verbatim capability descriptions, so discovery does not regress. Group RiskClass = strictest member; gating stays in the member handlers. The 25 flat names remain dispatchable as deprecated aliases (unadvertised), so existing clients keep working.","New test `internal/mcp/surface_budget_test.go` asserts the advertised tool count (6) and the serialized tools/list byte cost, failing if the surface grows without a retirement.","AC4 (process): no twenty-sixth capability was added in this wave; every new handle is a router over an existing capability."],"role":"implementation","summary":"M4 (sto:retrieval-surface-budget) is complete. The MCP advertised surface is grouped behind six capability handles, the flat tool names survive as dispatch aliases, and a test pins both the tool count and the schema byte cost so the surface cannot grow silently. The prefix cost was measured before and after as numbers.","tests":["Measured BEFORE (25 flat tools): ToolCount 25, AdvertisedToolCount 26, serialized advertised tool array 27981 bytes. AFTER (6 groups): ToolCount 6, AdvertisedToolCount 6, 22158 bytes — a ~5.8 KB (21%) reduction in the tools/list prefix cost.","Parity tests: a group call and its flat alias produce byte-identical content for read, local-write and canonical-write members; all 25 flat names remain dispatch aliases; canonical-write/external gating fires identically via group op and flat alias.","eka-mcp: `go test ./... -count=1` green; `anvil pipeline ci` success (format, vet, cross-compile, -race unit tests). Release v1.16.0 tagged; pins eka-core v1.13.0."]}